<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saale</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saale is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 413 km long, which rises in the Fichtelgebirge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and flows north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Halle, to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it marked the boundary between Germans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, and from the 10th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> marches and bishoprics extended east of it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saale.jpg" alt="Saale" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Grauesel at German Wikipedia. (Original text: Grauesel) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petersgrat_bei_Hof_Bayern.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Saale is also known as the Saxon Saale or the Thuringian Saale, which distinguishes it from the smaller Franconian Saale, a right-bank tributary of the Main, and from the Saale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a>. Its name comes from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning marsh, and it may also be related to a root for salt. The Slavic name Solawa, still found in Sorbian texts, comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a> words for salt and water.</p><p>The river rises on the slope of the Großer Waldstein mountain near Zell in the Fichtel Mountains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-franconia">Upper Franconia</a>, at an elevation of 728 metres. It pursues a winding course northward, passes the manufacturing town of Hof and enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, where it runs among the wooded low mountains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> until it reaches the valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolstadt">Rudolstadt</a> it receives the Schwarza, in whose valley lies the ruined castle of Schwarzburg, the ancestral seat of the formerly ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-schwarzburg">House of Schwarzburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> the Saale crosses the limestone hill region north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> and passes the university town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> it flows past the spa of Bad Kösen, receives the navigable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a>, and continues past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weißenfels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, Halle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a> and Calbe. It joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> just above Barby after 413 kilometres, a course shortened by 14 kilometres by a bypass from a natural length of 427 kilometres.</p><p>The river is navigable from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a>, and an unfinished canal was planned to connect it from Leuna with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">White Elster</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. The soil of the lower valley is exceptionally fertile and produces large quantities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> beet. The valley down to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> has many castles on the enclosing heights. Among its tributaries are the White Elster, the Ilm, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a>, the Salza, the Wipper and the Bode.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saale” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their homeland east of the Rhine is uncertain: one common proposal is the Mainfranken region of northwestern Bavaria, and archaeology links them and their Suebian neighbours with the Grossromstedter culture of the middle Elbe and <b>Saale</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The archbishops ruled a territory along the middle Elbe and <b>Saale</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of Barby held lands on the Elbe near the mouth of the <b>Saale</b>, between Magdeburg and Anhalt, from the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-barby">County of Barby</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory came to stretch from the Danube near Raetia north into Thuringia and the <b>Saale</b> region.</blockquote><a href="/state/hermunduri">Hermunduri</a></li><li><blockquote>The advocates of Weida, imperial officials whose men were all named Henry, governed lands on the upper Elster and <b>Saale</b> for the Hohenstaufen emperors.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-plauen">Lordship of Plauen</a></li><li><blockquote>The Schönburgs were imperial ministerials from the <b>Saale</b> region near Naumburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordships-of-schonburg">Lordships of Schönburg</a></li></ul></section>
